+# QA report: "You're subscribed" but no email arrives
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page tells the user **"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."** even when the server **rejects** the address. Nothing is stored, so no email is ever sent. The user isn't told anything went wrong.
+
+**Cause:** `public/app.js:8-14` awaits `fetch('/api/subscribe', …)` but never looks at the response. `fetch` only rejects on network failure, so a `422` with `{ "error": … }` falls through to the success message and `form.reset()` also clears the address the user typed.
+
+## Who hits it
+
+The browser's own `type="email"` check lets these addresses through, but `server.js` `validate()` rejects them with 422:
+
+| Address entered | API response | Page shows |
+|---|---|---|
+| `jane@example.com` | 201 `{ok:true}` | "You're subscribed…" ✅ |
+| `jane+news@example.com` | 422 "Addresses with + are not supported." | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+| `jane@example.c` (1-letter TLD, typo) | 422 "Enter a valid email address." | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+
+Plus-addresses (Gmail/Fastmail-style tags) are the most likely real-world trigger. Mistyped TLDs that the browser accepts also hit it.
+
+## How it was verified
+
+- Real browser: headless Chromium via Playwright, against `node server.js` running the current code. I filled the **Email** field, clicked **Subscribe**, captured the `/api/subscribe` response, and read the `role="status"` text.
+- No console errors or page errors during the flow. The only failed request is the expected 422, which the page ignores.
+
+## Test: `e2e/subscribe.test.js`
+
+Run it with `node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js`. The test starts `server.js` on a random port and then drives Chromium.
+
+- **Valid address → success message, field cleared.** Passes now.
+- **`jane+news@example.com` → shows the server's error, not "You're subscribed", and keeps the address in the field.** **Fails now.**
+- **`jane@example.c` → same assertions.** **Fails now.**
+
+Current result: 1 pass, 2 fail, with `UI claimed success although the API returned 422` / actual `"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."`
+
+**Checking that the test has teeth:** I temporarily patched `app.js` to check `res.ok` and show `error`. All 3 tests passed. I then reverted the patch, and `git status` shows `app.js` unchanged. So the tests fail because of this bug and pass once it is fixed, which rules out a broken test setup.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+In `public/app.js`, check `res.ok`. On failure, show the `error` from the response body and don't reset the form. A separate product question: should `+` addresses be rejected at all? `server.js:10` rejects them on purpose, but that blocks a common, valid address style.
+
+## Other observations (not covered by the test)
+
+- The server keeps subscribers in an in-memory `Set` and sends no email anywhere in this codebase. A successful signup is lost on restart, and the "Check your inbox" promise depends on something outside this repo. Not verified: whether any mail sending exists elsewhere.
